[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Management and Garbage Collection in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Efficient memory management is crucial for developing high-performance Android applications. Because mobile devices have constrained hardware resources, understanding how Java allocates memory and how the Android runtime reclaims it helps prevent application crashes, lag, and poor user experiences."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Android Memory Model"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Android applications run in the Dalvik Virtual Machine or Android Runtime (ART), which manages memory allocation using the standard Java heap. When you instantiate objects using the "new" keyword, they are allocated on the heap. The operating system assigns a strict memory limit per application process based on the device's total RAM."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Heap Memory: Where all class instances and arrays are dynamically allocated."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stack Memory: Stores primitive local variables and references to objects residing on the heap."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Native Heap: Used by C/C++ libraries and Android graphics components (like Bitmaps)."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"How Garbage Collection Works"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Garbage Collection (GC) is the automated process of identifying and discarding objects that are no longer needed by the application, thereby freeing up heap space. ART uses generational garbage collection, categorizing objects into different buckets (young generation and old generation) based on their lifespan."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mark and Sweep: The GC traverses the object graph starting from root nodes (like active threads, static variables, and local variables) and marks all reachable objects. Unmarked objects are considered garbage and are swept away."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Concurrent GC: ART performs GC concurrently with application threads to minimize 'stop-the-world' pauses, though heavy allocation can still trigger noticeable UI stutters (jank)."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Memory Leaks in Android"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"A memory leak occurs when an object is no longer needed by the application, but a reference to it prevents the Garbage Collector from reclaiming its memory. In Android, the most common memory leak involves Activities and Contexts."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public class LeakyActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    private static Drawable leakedDrawable;

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_leaky);

        // BAD: A static field holds a reference to the Activity's Context
        leakedDrawable = getResources().getDrawable(R.drawable.banner);
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the static variable "leakedDrawable" outlives the lifecycle of the Activity. Because Drawables can hold implicit references to their parent Context, the entire Activity instance remains in memory even after the user exits, causing a severe memory leak."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Memory Optimization"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid Static Contexts: Never store Activity or View references in static variables. Use Application context when global lifecycle access is required."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be Careful with Inner Classes: Non-static inner classes (like Threads, Handlers, and AsyncTask instances) hold an implicit reference to their outer class. Use static inner classes with WeakReference instead."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Release Resources Properly: Clean up listeners, cursors, and database connections in the onPause() or onDestroy() lifecycle methods."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Optimize Bitmaps: Images consume vast amounts of memory. Scale down large bitmaps to match the target ImageView dimensions using BitmapFactory.Options."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Effective memory management ensures your Android app runs smoothly and avoids OutOfMemoryError (OOM) crashes. By utilizing tools like Android Studio Profiler and LeakCanary, you can proactively detect and resolve memory leaks throughout your development lifecycle."}]
